WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.400
If you’re anything like me,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.560
you have lots of ambitious
plans for the future.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.160
I’ve got a whole series of
new books I want to write,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.400
thousands more
videos to script.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.360
But guess what?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.760
None of us may be able to do
any of the things we want to do

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.240
if we don't have our health.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.480
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.040
Today we take a close look at
the effectiveness of sunscreen

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.800
and we start with the results
of a randomized controlled trial

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.216
to see if it can actually
slow skin aging.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.280
Considered the single most
important practice

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.240
for maintaining youthful skin:

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.000
the daily application
of sunscreen

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.720
and taking
other protective measures,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.480
like wearing a hat.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.240
All the other things
you can do for your skin

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.720
pale in comparison,

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.600
especially for those
with pale skin.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.120
Up to 90 percent
of visible facial aging

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.520
among people
with lighter skin tones

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.560
is due to exposure to sunlight.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.720
Those with darker skin
are relatively protected

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.080
due to their built-in
melanin sunscreen,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.520
but they're still affected.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.440
Sun damage in darker skin
tends to be less about wrinkles

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.520
and more
about pigmentation issues,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.760
such as uneven skin tone,
dark patches,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.240
and small dark bumps
on the face.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.040
Either way,
dermatologists now agree

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.800
that there is
nothing more important

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.400
to slow the signs of aging

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.225
than to protect your skin
from the sun.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.360
Factors like sun exposure
and smoking

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.120
can make us look
up to 11 years older.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.280
Compare that to extensive
cosmetic surgeryâ€”

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.560
a face lift and a neck lift,
and removing excess skin

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.680
from both the upper
and lower eyelids,

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.760
and a forehead lift,
which combined,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.880
can make us look
about eight years younger.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.200
So, a healthy lifestyle
may work even better.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.080
Protecting your skin
from the sun

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.080
should be a lifelong endeavor.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.760
This can involve
applying sunscreen,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.960
wearing sun-protective clothing,
hats, and sunglasses,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.240
and avoiding direct sunlight

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.640
during the peak hours
of 10:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.760
and instead
seeking shady, covered areas.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.240
Sunbathing is frowned upon,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.040
even with sunscreens
like zinc oxide

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.920
or titanium dioxide that offer
broad-spectrum protection

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.440
against both UVA
and UVB rays.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.600
We now know
that other wavelengths,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.880
such as near infrared,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.480
that are not covered
by sunscreens

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.880
also contribute to skin aging.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.080
Men and women
who use tanning beds

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.440
appear significantly older
than those who don't,

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.840
and white women who sunbathe
appear years older

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.800
than they actually are,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.720
similar to what is seen
with smoking.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.360
UVA rays are primarily
responsible for skin aging,

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.240
whereas UVB are the rays
that cause sunburn,

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.360
but a broad-spectrum sunscreen
covering both is recommended

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:38.000
since both types of UV
contribute to cancer risk.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.240
To prevent skin cancer,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.240
the American Academy
of Dermatology recommends

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.400
sunscreen with an SPF of 30
or higher,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.640
but an SPF even as low as 15
can help prevent skin aging.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.080
How do we know?

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.800
Because it's been
put to the test.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:55.920
Despite the widespread belief

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.400
that the use of sunscreen
would prevent skin aging,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.400
all we had was data
on hairless mice.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.160
That is, until ten years ago.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.720
Nine hundred adults
were randomized

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.600
either to years
of daily sunscreen use

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.080
or to continue with
their own discretionary use.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.880
(It was considered unethical
to withhold protection

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.320
by giving people
placebo sunscreen.)

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.040
In the end, 77 percent

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.680
in the recommended
daily sunscreen group

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.680
were applying sunscreen

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.800
at least three to four days
per week

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.080
compared with only 33 percent

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.120
in the discretionary use group.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.640
Would that be
enough of a difference

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:29.760
to make a difference?

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.400
Yes, there was
significantly less skin aging

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.400
in the instructed
daily use group.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.480
In fact, they suffered

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.360
no detectable increase
in skin aging

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.280
over the four-and-a-half-year
study.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.600
The researchers concluded
"Regular sunscreen use

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.480
retards skin aging in healthy,
middle-aged men and women."

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:47.520
In our next story,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:53.880
sunscreen is put to the test to see
if it can actually prevent skin cancer.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.920
Decades ago,
studies started bubbling up,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.920
reporting that not only
was the use of sunscreen

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.000
not found to be protective
against skin cancer,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.680
but it was associated with
significantly elevated risk.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.920
But subsequent studies offered
the obvious explanation

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.360
of this paradox.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.240
People who use sunscreen are
more likely to be sunbathers.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.760
That's like finding those
who use motorcycle helmets

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.840
are more likely to die
on the highway.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.120
It's not that sunscreen
and helmets aren't protective;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.360
it's just
that they're associated

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.240
with engagement
in risky activities.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.520
But you don't know,
until you put it to the test.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.000
As I noted in my last video,
randomized controlled trials

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.800
have shown that regular use
of sunscreen

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.360
can arrest visible signs
of skin aging,

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.240
including
biopsy-proven reductions

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.320
in UV-related skin damage.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.760
But are there
interventional trials

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.880
proving sunscreen
can prevent cancer?

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:43.440
Yes.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.800
Organ transplant recipients

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.920
are highly susceptible
to skin cancers

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.240
because they have to be given
immune suppressants

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.280
to prevent organ rejection.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.360
A group of 120 organ recipients

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:56.160
were equally informed
about sun protection measures,

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.840
but half were given free

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.080
broad spectrum
SPF 50+ sunscreen

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.120
for daily application
to their head, neck,

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.680
forearms, and hands.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.880
After two years, there were
nine new basal carcinomas

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.480
in the control group
versus only two

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:09.640
in the sunscreen group.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.480
That sounds good, but it may
have just been a fluke.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.040
In contrast, there was
a highly significant difference

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.200
in the proportion of patients

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.800
who got new invasive
squamous cell carcinomas.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.320
Eight new cases
in the control group

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.320
versus zero
in the sunscreen group.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.520
But wait until you hear
about what happened

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.080
to their actinic keratoses,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.400
the precancerous growths
that can turn into skin cancer.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.840
They all started out
covered in them,

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.760
a total of 191 detected
in each group.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.200
In the subsequent 24 months,

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.200
the control group
developed 82 new ones.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:45.120
How many did
the sunscreen group develop?

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.560
Negative 102.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.880
More than 100
precancerous growths

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.600
in the sunscreen group
spontaneously regressed

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:56.200
and vanished completely
(compared to 82 new growths

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.400
developing in the control group

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.280
without a single one
disappearing).

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.440
Their bodies, even in
their immunocompromised state,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.200
could heal itself
once it just stopped

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.840
being bombarded with
so many cancer-causing rays.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.200
Other randomized
controlled trials

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.360
have shown similar findings,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.800
but generalizability
is limited.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.200
The studies all used
high-risk populations,

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.400
for example, those living
in subtropical climates,

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.480
or with a personal history
of precancerous lesions.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.580
So, cancer prevention efficacy
in the general population

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.920
would be expected to be less.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.920
On the other hand, the studies
could also have underestimated

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.520
the impact since they were
relatively short-term

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:34.680
(not exceeding four years)

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.640
and, ethically, had to allow
the control group participants

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.640
to slather on
their own sunscreen,

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.360
which would dilute
the difference between groups.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.080
For maximum effectiveness,

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:46.680
sunscreen needs to be
applied properly,

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.600
which apparently rarely happens
in the real world.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.320
In a study of nearly 5,000
skiers and snowboarders,

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.560
almost no one,

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.880
only about 4 percent
were fully compliant

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:57.200
with sunscreen recommendations.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.880
First, there's the amount.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.760
The FDA standard
is two milligrams

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.600
per square centimeter.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.080
What does that mean?

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.160
Use the teaspoon rule:

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.960
one teaspoon for your face,
head and neck,

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.600
one for the front
of your torso,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.840
another for your back,
one teaspoon for each arm

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.680
and two teaspoons for each leg.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.640
That's a total
of nine teaspoons.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.040
That's about
the total volume equivalent

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.320
of a golf ball or shot glass,
to help you visualize it.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.280
Unfortunately,
the average sunscreen user

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.280
may only apply a quarter
of the recommended amount.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.000
This is why high SPF sunscreens
are often recommended,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.200
like 50+ SPF.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.640
Although the FDA recommends
a minimum SPF of only 15

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.200
to prevent skin cancer,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.440
under normal
consumer conditions,

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:41.560
even an SPF of 50
may effectively give you

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.440
only an SPF of 12.5,

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.280
because most people
don't put on enough.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:47.400
Randomized double-blind,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.320
head-to-head,
split-face experiments,

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.520
where you're randomized
to apply one sunscreen

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:52.520
to one side of your face

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.400
and another sunscreen
to the other side,

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.320
show that in real life use,

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.320
SPF 100+ sunscreen works
significantly better

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.640
than SPF 50+ sunscreen.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.480
More than 50 percent
of the participants

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.600
were sunburned more
on their SPF 50+ side

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.640
compared to only
about 5 percent

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.400
on their SPF 100+ side.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.840
Now, a potential downside
of higher SPF sunscreens

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.680
is that they could provide
a false sense of security.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.520
Those randomized
to an SPF 30 sunscreen

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.880
ended up spending
more cumulative time in the sun

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.400
than those receiving
an SPF 10 sunscreen,

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.600
as much as five times longer
in some cases.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:29.560
What else?

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:31.480
Well, people tend to remember
to use sunscreen

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:33.080
on a sunny day at the beach,

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.560
but sun protection is needed
even on cloudy days,

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.080
since the UV rays
are not dampened

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.120
as much as visible light.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.160
There's even a phenomenon
known as "cloud enhancement"

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.120
where overcast skies
can sometimes result

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.520
in even more UV reaching
the Earth's surface

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:48.240
compared to clear skies.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.080
Sunscreen labels often suggest
waiting at least 15 minutes

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.520
after application
before going outdoors,

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:54.800
but when put to the test,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.040
sunscreen was found
to start working immediately

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:59.200
with the full effect apparent
by minute ten.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.800
However, if water resistance
is required,

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:02.800
it may be prudent to wait

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.280
the full 15 to 30 minutes
after application

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.400
before taking a dip.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.600
The terms "waterproof"
or "sweatproof"

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.520
appear to be
meaningless marketing,

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.200
as no difference
in retention was noted

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.160
between "waterproof" products
and those that were

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.280
merely labeled
"water-resistant."

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:19.400
Both were better than

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.280
non-water-resistant products,
though,

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.520
which lost nearly
all their protective effects

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:26.880
within 20 minutes
of water immersion.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.080
The suggestion that sunscreen
should be reapplied

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.360
every two to three hours
only applies under conditions

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.760
in which it is rinsed off
by water or sweat,

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.120
or rubbed off by friction
from clothing or sand.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:40.560
Even after allowing sunscreen
to dry first for 20 minutes,

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.440
between 15 and 60 percent
of its protective effect

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.680
can be lost
after contact with sand.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:48.760
Otherwise, if the recommended
amount is applied

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.680
and the sunscreen layer
is not disturbed,

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.880
SPF can be maintained
for as long as eight hours.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:55.640
Finally today,

00:09:55.640 --> 00:10:00.800
the FDA has concluded that only two sunscreen
ingredients can be considered safe:

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.880
zinc oxide and titanium dioxide.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.080
What kind of sunscreen
should you use?

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.160
Cream-based is preferable
to spray-on,

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.640
since the completeness
of application

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.720
is more readily visualized.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:13.080
To help with adequate coverage,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:14.400
spray-on sunscreens should be

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.000
rubbed on immediately
after spraying.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.040
Aerosolized sunscreens
are flammable

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.160
and can combust on skin
upon exposure to an open flame,

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.920
even after the sunscreen
has dried.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.800
What's more,
the safety of breathing in

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:28.280
aerosolized sunscreen chemicals

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:30.800
has not been
adequately studied,

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.760
though frankly the same thing
could be said

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.280
about rubbing them
on your skin.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.360
The concerns
surrounding sunscreen safety

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.920
are three-fold:

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:41.200
increased
intentional sun exposure,

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.120
vitamin D deficiency,
and untoward effects

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:47.120
from systemic absorption
of sunscreen chemicals.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:50.920
In the 1800s, we first learned
that UVB caused sunburns

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.320
and formulated sunscreens
to block it.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:54.640
Nearly a century later,
we learned

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.640
about the contribution of UVA.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.280
Now, a full century later,

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.760
we are realizing that infrared
and visible light

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:02.320
may also be contributing
to cancer

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.720
and premature skin aging.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.200
Sunlight comes to us
in three major bands:

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:09.840
visible light, ultraviolet,
and infrared.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.480
Visible light includes
the colors of the rainbow—

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:14.960
red, orange, yellow, green,
blue, indigo, and violet,

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.760
or ROYGBIV.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:20.800
Ultraviolet—UV—is to the right
of violet in ROYGBIV,

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.520
and infrared
to the left of red.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.360
Sunscreen can protect us

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:27.800
against the free radical damage
of UV,

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:29.600
but half
of the free radical formation

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:30.680
in our skin from the sun

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:33.160
may be from the visible
or infrared spectrums,

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:36.360
which sunscreens
may not adequately cover.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:40.120
So, that's where sun avoidance
strategies come in,

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.040
but you can imagine
how shade-seeking

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.080
could be undermined
by the solacement

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.840
of a sunscreen
security blanket.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:48.640
What about vitamin D?

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:50.680
The theoretical concern
about sunscreen

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:51.920
affecting vitamin D status

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:54.480
does not seem to manifest
in the real world,

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.760
likely because
the UV dose necessary

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.280
for vitamin D production
is so low,

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.560
well before skin
turns a pinker shade.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:05.040
However, concerns
about the systemic absorption

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.440
of sunscreen chemicals
were underscored

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.720
by the recent FDA bombshell

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:12.640
that not a single one
of sunscreen chemicals

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.240
in current use
can be considered

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:16.960
generally recognized as safe.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.280
Only two active ingredients
got the green light,

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.240
the two non-chemical
"mineral" sunscreens:

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:24.800
titanium dioxide
and zinc oxide.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.640
The revelation was based
on a growing body of evidence

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.320
that transdermal, meaning
through-the-skin, absorption

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:31.640
of sunscreen chemicals

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:34.320
was greater than
we previously thought,

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.360
raising unevaluated
safety concerns.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.080
Unevaluated, because we
previously didn't think so much

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:41.840
got into our bloodstreams.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:43.880
In 2019, a study published

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:45.560
in the Journal of
the American Medical Association

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.720
found that under maximum
usage conditions

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:49.600
over a period of days,
the blood levels

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:51.640
of all the sunscreen chemicals
they tested

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.280
exceeded the FDA threshold

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.680
that could potentially waive
further safety testing.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:13:00.280
Then, in 2020, they found
that just a single application

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:02.200
of all the chemical sunscreens
they tested

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:04.360
surpassed the threshold.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.760
Now, just because they're
absorbed into our system,

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.160
doesn't necessarily mean
they're unsafe.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.640
It just means they need
to be tested for safety,

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.080
which, the FDA determined,

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.480
the multibillion-dollar
sunscreen industry

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.840
has so far failed to do.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:19.600
The FDA concluded
that two ingredients

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:20.720
can be considered safe—

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:23.360
zinc oxide
and titanium dioxide,

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.720
and two can be
considered unsafe—

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.160
P.A.B.A.
and trolamine salicylate.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.520
And the other 12 ingredients
that are currently marketed

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.440
have yet to be
sufficiently safety tested.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.960
According to a review of more
than 700 sunscreen brands,

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:38.800
it appears P.A.B.A.
and trolamine

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:41.080
are no longer being marketed
in sunscreens

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:42.760
sold in the United States,

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:45.320
but they still may be available
in other countries.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:46.920
Until the data are in
on the rest

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.120
of the chemical sunscreens,

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:51.800
I would recommend sticking
to the two mineral sunscreens.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:54.720
Historically, they've tended
to be thicker and whiter,

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.400
which could lead
to even more severe underdosing,

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.360
but newer
micronized formulations

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.240
with smaller
mineral particle sizes

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.920
tend to be less noticeable.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:06.760
This is particularly an issue
for those with darker skin.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.320
The average built-in SPF
of black skin

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:12.600
(also known in the medical
literature as "ethnic skin"

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:18.040
or "SOC," skin of color)
is about 13 naturally,

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.800
compared with only about three
for white skin.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.640
Though there haven't been
any interventional studies

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.560
on sunscreen effectiveness
for skin cancer prevention

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.360
in people with dark skin,

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:31.360
SPF 13 is not considered
sufficient sun protection;

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:33.560
so, the American Academy
of Dermatology recommends

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:36.240
regular sunscreen use
with an SPF of 30 or higher

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.320
for people of all skin types.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:40.920
Unfortunately,
only about 12 percent

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:43.600
of non-Hispanic Blacks
and 31 percent of Hispanics

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.640
report regularly using
sunscreen,

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:49.200
compared to about 44 percent
of non-Hispanic whites.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.520
Despite this,
the incidence of melanoma,

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:53.560
the deadliest skin cancer,
is five times lower

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.080
in Hispanics compared to whites

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.880
and more than 25 times lower
among Blacks.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:00.840
However, the mortality rate
if you do get it

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:02.800
is higher among
African Americans,

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.554
presumed to be due
from delayed diagnosis.

